\title[A Sharp $1/4$ Threshold for Unit-Distance Graphs]{The Independence Ratio and the Fractional Chromatic Number:\\ A Sharp $1/4$ Threshold for Unit-Distance Graphs}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We develop, in full rigour, the finite-graph combinatorics that connects the
\emph{independence ratio} of a graph to lower bounds on its integral and
fractional chromatic numbers, and we explain how this machinery bears on the
fractional chromatic number of the Euclidean plane. For a finite graph $G$ on
$n>0$ vertices with independence number $\ind(G)$, the independence ratio is
$i(G)=\ind(G)/n$. We prove the integral pigeonhole bound $n\le k\,\ind(G)$ for
every proper $k$-colouring, deduce that $i(G)<1/4$ forces $G$ to be
non-$4$-colourable with chromatic number $\chi(G)>4$, and show this threshold is
sharp via the complete graph $K_4$, whose ratio is exactly $1/4$. We then
formulate fractional colourings as nonnegative weightings of the independent
sets subject to a covering constraint, prove the linear-programming lower bound
$\val\ge n/\ind(G)$ by double counting, and conclude the fractional analogue:
$i(G)<1/4$ implies that \emph{every} fractional colouring has value greater than
$4$, i.e.\ $\chi_f(G)>4$. Because any finite unit-distance graph embeds in the
plane, the existence of such a gadget forces $\chi_f(\R^2)>4$. We anchor the
scale with the unit equilateral triangle (ratio exactly $1/3$) and record a
complementary Tur\'an/Caro--Wei route to independence-ratio bounds.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

The \emph{Hadwiger--Nelson problem} asks for the least number of colours needed
to colour the Euclidean plane so that no two points at distance exactly $1$ share
a colour. This quantity, the chromatic number of the plane $\chi(\R^2)$, was
known for decades to lie between $4$ and $7$, and in 2018 was shown to be at
least $5$ by an explicit finite unit-distance graph requiring five colours.

A closely related and more tractable invariant is the \emph{fractional chromatic
number} of the plane, $\chi_f(\R^2)$, which relaxes the integrality of colour
assignments. It had long been conjectured that $\chi_f(\R^2)=4$, and, in an
equivalent finite-gadget formulation, that no finite unit-distance graph in the
plane can have independence ratio strictly below $1/4$. Erd\H{o}s asked in 1987
whether such a sub-$1/4$ gadget exists; a positive answer refutes the conjecture
and forces $\chi_f(\R^2)>4$.

This paper does not construct the geometric gadget; rather, it isolates and
proves the \emph{reduction} that gives the frontier its meaning. The threshold
$1/4$ is not an artefact of geometry but a transparent consequence of two
counting arguments---one integral and one fractional---each a single application
of the pigeonhole principle. The fraction $1/4$ is exactly the reciprocal of the
conjectured value $4$, so ``$i(G)<1/4$'' is literally ``$n/\ind(G)>4$.''

\section{Definitions}

Throughout, $G$ is a finite simple graph on a vertex set $V$ with $n=|V|>0$. We
write $u\sim v$ when $u$ and $v$ are adjacent.

\begin{definition}[Independent set]
A set $S\subseteq V$ is \emph{independent} if for all $u,v\in S$ with $u\ne v$ we
have $u\not\sim v$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Independence number and ratio]
The \emph{independence number} $\ind(G)$ is the maximum cardinality of an
independent set. The \emph{independence ratio} is $i(G)=\ind(G)/n\in(0,1]$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Proper colouring]
A \emph{proper $k$-colouring} is a map $C:V\to\{1,\dots,k\}$ with
$u\sim v\Rightarrow C(u)\ne C(v)$. The \emph{chromatic number} $\chi(G)$ is the
least such $k$; $G$ is \emph{$k$-colourable} if $\chi(G)\le k$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Fractional colouring]
Let $\mathcal{I}$ be the family of all subsets of $V$. A \emph{fractional
colouring} is a weight $w:\mathcal{I}\to\Q_{\ge0}$ with:
\emph{(nonnegativity)} $w(s)\ge0$;
\emph{(support)} $w(s)>0$ only if $s$ is independent;
\emph{(covering)} $\sum_{s\ni v}w(s)\ge1$ for every $v\in V$.
Its \emph{value} is $\val(w)=\sum_s w(s)$, and $\chi_f(G)=\inf_w \val(w)$.
\end{definition}

A proper $k$-colouring induces the fractional colouring assigning weight $1$ to
each of its $k$ colour classes; its value is $k$, so $\chi_f(G)\le\chi(G)$.

\section{The integral colour-class bound and the $1/4$ threshold}

\begin{theorem}[Colour-class bound]\label{thm:colourclass}
If $G$ admits a proper $k$-colouring, then $n\le k\,\ind(G)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For each colour $c$, let $V_c=\{v:C(v)=c\}$. Properness makes $V_c$ independent,
so $|V_c|\le\ind(G)$. The classes partition $V$, hence
$n=\sum_{c=1}^k |V_c|\le\sum_{c=1}^k \ind(G)=k\,\ind(G)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Large colour class]
If $k>0$ and $C$ is a proper $k$-colouring, some colour class has cardinality at
least $n/k$; equivalently there is an independent set $S$ with $n\le k\,|S|$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
The $k$ class sizes sum to $n$, so by averaging one is at least $n/k$; that class
is independent by properness.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Four colours impossible below the threshold]\label{thm:notfour}
If $n>0$ and $i(G)<1/4$, then $G$ is not $4$-colourable.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $G$ were $4$-colourable, Theorem~\ref{thm:colourclass} with $k=4$ gives
$n\le4\,\ind(G)$, i.e.\ $i(G)\ge1/4$, contradicting $i(G)<1/4$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Chromatic number exceeds four]\label{thm:chigt4}
If $n>0$ and $i(G)<1/4$, then $\chi(G)>4$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\chi(G)\le4$ is equivalent to $4$-colourability, excluded by
Theorem~\ref{thm:notfour}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sharpness]\label{thm:sharp}
For every $k\ge1$, the complete graph $K_k$ is $k$-colourable and $i(K_k)=1/k$.
In particular $K_4$ is $4$-colourable with independence ratio exactly $1/4$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Giving each vertex its own colour is proper, so $K_k$ is $k$-colourable. Every
pair is adjacent, so $\ind(K_k)=1$ and $i(K_k)=1/k$.
\end{proof}

\section{Fractional colourings and the LP lower bound}

\begin{theorem}[LP lower bound]\label{thm:lp}
Let $w$ be a fractional colouring of $G$ with $\ind(G)>0$. Then
$\val(w)\ge n/\ind(G)$, and hence $\chi_f(G)\ge n/\ind(G)=1/i(G)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Consider $\sum_{v\in V}\sum_{s\ni v}w(s)$. Summing first over $v$ and using the
covering constraint at each of the $n$ vertices gives
$\sum_{v}\sum_{s\ni v}w(s)\ge n$. Exchanging the order of summation counts each
set $s$ once per contained vertex, so
$\sum_{v}\sum_{s\ni v}w(s)=\sum_s w(s)\,|s|$. By support, $w(s)>0$ only for
independent $s$, where $|s|\le\ind(G)$; since $w(s)\ge0$,
$\sum_s w(s)\,|s|\le\ind(G)\sum_s w(s)=\ind(G)\,\val(w)$. Chaining gives
$n\le\ind(G)\,\val(w)$; divide by $\ind(G)>0$ and take the infimum over $w$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Fractional frontier]\label{thm:fracfrontier}
If $n>0$ and $i(G)<1/4$, then $\chi_f(G)>4$; indeed every fractional colouring of
$G$ has value strictly greater than $4$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
From $i(G)<1/4$ with $n>0$ we get $n/\ind(G)>4$ (with $\ind(G)\ge1>0$). By
Theorem~\ref{thm:lp} every fractional colouring has value at least
$n/\ind(G)>4$.
\end{proof}

Since $\chi_f(G)\le\chi(G)$, Theorem~\ref{thm:fracfrontier} implies
Theorem~\ref{thm:chigt4}.

\begin{remark}[Trivial fractional colouring]
Weight $1$ on every singleton $\{v\}$ and $0$ elsewhere is a valid fractional
colouring of value $n$, so $\chi_f(G)\le n$ always.
\end{remark}

\section{Unit-distance graphs and the plane}

\begin{definition}[Unit-distance graph]
Given points $p:\iota\to\R^2$ with $\iota$ finite, the \emph{unit-distance graph}
$U(p)$ has vertex set $\iota$ and $i\sim j$ iff $i\ne j$ and
$\lVert p_i-p_j\rVert=1$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Equilateral triangle]\label{prop:tri}
If $p$ realises an equilateral triangle of side $1$, then $U(p)=K_3$,
$\ind(U(p))=1$, and $i(U(p))=1/3$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
All three pairwise distances equal $1$, so $U(p)=K_3$; any independent set has at
most one vertex, giving $\ind=1$ and $i=1/3$.
\end{proof}

The value $1/3>1/4$ fixes the natural scale: the smallest nondegenerate
unit-distance clique already lies above the frontier.

Any fractional colouring of the plane restricts to a fractional colouring of any
finite unit-distance subgraph $U(p)$, so
\[
\chi_f(\R^2)\ge\chi_f(U(p))\ge\frac{|\iota|}{\ind(U(p))}=\frac{1}{i(U(p))}.
\]

\begin{corollary}[Sub-$1/4$ gadget]\label{cor:gadget}
If there exists a finite $p$ in the plane with $i(U(p))<1/4$, then
$\chi_f(\R^2)>4$, refuting $\chi_f(\R^2)=4$ and answering Erd\H{o}s's 1987
question affirmatively.
\end{corollary}

The threshold is sharp for the target: $1/4$ is the reciprocal of the conjectured
value $\chi_f(\R^2)=4$.

\section{A complementary edge-count bound}

\begin{theorem}[Tur\'an/Caro--Wei route]\label{thm:turan}
Let $G$ have $n$ vertices and $m\ge1$ edges. Then $G$ has an independent set $S$
with $|S|\ge n^2/(2m+n)$, equivalently $i(G)\ge n/(2m+n)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Caro--Wei gives $\ind(G)\ge\sum_v \tfrac{1}{d(v)+1}$; by the AM--HM inequality
this is at least $n^2/\sum_v(d(v)+1)=n^2/(2m+n)$, using $\sum_v d(v)=2m$.
\end{proof}

For a graph that is both $4$-colourable and sparse,
$i(G)\ge\max\!\big(\tfrac14,\ n/(2m+n)\big)$. Forcing $n/(2m+n)<1/4$ requires
$m>3n/2$, so any sub-$1/4$ gadget must have average degree exceeding $3$.

\section{Discussion}

The hard component of the Hadwiger--Nelson circle is geometric: constructing a
finite planar unit-distance configuration whose independence ratio dips below
$1/4$. The \emph{reduction}---turning such a configuration into a lower bound on
$\chi_f(\R^2)$---is elementary and is captured by
Theorems~\ref{thm:colourclass}, \ref{thm:lp}, and \ref{thm:fracfrontier}. The
threshold $1/4$ is the reciprocal of $4$; the fractional bound is strictly
stronger than the integral one and is the relevant one for the plane; and
sharpness is genuine, realised by $K_4$.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Threshold sharpness]
There is a sequence of finite planar unit-distance graphs whose independence
ratios converge to $1/4$ from above, but none attains $1/4$; the infimum is
approached but never met. The ratio behaves like a supremal packing density: each
constraint shaves it by a rational amount, so the extremal value is a limit of
rationals.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Tight duality for vertex-transitive gadgets]
For every finite vertex-transitive unit-distance graph, $\chi_f=n/\ind$ with no
gap; a vertex-transitive planar gadget with $i<1/4$ then gives the cleanest
certificate that $\chi_f(\R^2)>4$. Symmetry averages any fractional colouring into
a uniform one, collapsing the duality gap.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[A $1/4-\varepsilon$ barrier scales the plane]
If finite planar unit-distance graphs exist with independence ratio at most
$1/4-\varepsilon$ for fixed $\varepsilon>0$, then
$\chi_f(\R^2)\ge \tfrac{1}{1/4-\varepsilon}=4+\tfrac{4\varepsilon}{1-4\varepsilon}$.
The reciprocal map amplifies additive ratio gains into strictly larger gains in
the chromatic lower bound.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Simplices are the worst anchors]
Among small unit-distance cliques, the complete graphs $K_k$ are the extremal
ratio-$1/k$ configurations, and no planar gadget built purely from triangle-rich
substructures can beat the frontier without introducing longer-range
non-adjacencies.
\end{conjecture}

\section{Conclusion}

We have given a self-contained account of the combinatorial reduction underlying
the fractional Hadwiger--Nelson problem: independence ratio below $1/4$ forces
the fractional chromatic number above $4$, for finite graphs and, by restriction,
for the plane. The argument rests on two double-counting inequalities, is sharp at
$K_4$, and is anchored by $i(K_3)=1/3$. What remains is the geometric construction
of a witness---a task this framework converts, mechanically, into quantitative
progress on the colouring of the plane.

\end{document}

